overing required symbols. So the objective is to minimize the number of retained nodes (or equivalently maximize removed nodes) subject to coverage. That is a typical \"minimum set cover\" but with forced non-removable nodes. Since non-removable are forced, we want to remove all removable nodes that are not strictly needed. If a required symbol is only present on removable nodes, we cannot remove all of them; we must keep at least one. To minimize retained nodes, we keep exactly one. So we need to pick which one to keep. Utilities might affect the decision if there is a tie, but not relevant. However, the problem might also require that the retained nodes form a \"valid tree\"? But no tree structure in output.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I think the safest is to implement a solution that:\n- Compute retained_node_ids as all nodes that are not removable.\n- For each required symbol that is not present in the set of non-removable nodes, find all removable nodes with that symbol and add the one with the highest utility to retained (if multiple, keep the one with highest utility to maximize something; but if objective is to minimize retained, we'd keep exactly one; but if objective is to maximize utility of retained, we might keep all of them. However, the fixtures required symbols are all covered by non-removable, so this edge case doesn't appear. I'll assume the objective is to maximize utility given that we must remove all removable nodes except those needed to cover missing required symbols. So we keep the highest utility one among the missing symbol's removable nodes. That seems plausible.\n\nBut there is also replacement candidates. Could be that we can replace a node with a different node? Is there a concept of \"replacement\"? The problem statement mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Maybe we are allowed to replace a node with another node that has same symbol? Not clear.\n\nGiven the limited information, I'll implement a simple algorithm:\n- Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build node_by_id dict.\n- Determine which nodes are mandatory (not removable).\n- For each required symbol, check if any mandatory node has that symbol. If not, we need to retain at least one removable node with that symbol. Choose one (maybe the one with highest utility, to maximize retained utility).\n- All other removable nodes are removed.\n- Return removed_node_ids as all ids not in retained.\n\nBut note: The root might not be in required_symbols? It is \"root\" symbol in fixtures. So we keep root anyway as mandatory.\n\nEdge case: If a node is removable but its parent is removed, does that affect anything? The output doesn't require tree validation, so probably not.\n\nThus implementation.\n\nNow produce Python code for solve(case). The function receives a payload (the case payload) with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Possibly also \"_case_nonce\" but ignore. Return dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nWrite code accordingly.\n\nThen produce JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source string.\n\nMake sure to escape properly.\n\nI'll write solve(case) as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    # initially retain all nodes that are not removable\n    retained = set()\n    removed = set()\n    for node in nodes:\n        if not node['removable']:\n            retained.add(node['id'])\n        else:\n            removed.add(node['id'])  # initially removed, will later conditionally keep some\n    # For each required symbol not covered by retained nodes, find removable nodes with that symbol\n    # Ensure we keep at least one.\n    covered_symbols = set()\n    for node_id in retained:\n        covered_symbols.add(node_map[node_id]['symbol'])\n    missing_symbols = required_symbols - covered_symbols\n    for sym in missing_symbols:\n        # find all removable nodes with this symbol\n        candidates = [node for node in nodes if node['symbol']==sym and node['removable']]\n        if candidates:\n            # choose the one with highest utility (to maximize retained utility)\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda x: x['utility'])\n            retained.add(best['id'])\n            removed.discard(best['id'])\n            covered_symbols.add(sym)\n    # If after this, there are still missing symbols, that would mean required symbol not present at all; but assume valid input.\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": sorted(list(removed)),\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained))\n    }\n\nBut note: The order of ids in output is not specified; we can sort for determinism.\n\nTest on fixture: For case a-000, retained: root, n1, n3, n5 (non-removable). removable: n2, n4, n6. required symbols root and s1 already covered (root has root, n1 has s1). So no missing symbols -> removed all three. That matches expectation.\n\nIf there were a missing symbol, e.g., required symbol 's3' only on removable node n7, then we would add n7 to retained.\n\nBut the problem might also involve \"replacement\" where if a node is removed, its children could be reattached to its parent? That would require restructuring the tree. Since output only requires ids, no, it's just selection.\n\nGiven the ambiguous description, I'll go with this simple algorithm. If the actual tests require a more complex optimization, the solution might fail, but it's the best we can infer from fixtures.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1928,"completion_tokens":4284,"total_tokens":6212,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4014},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":136},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}